The tech stack is a reference choice, not a description of any specific hospital's systems.

22
00:02:11.724 --> 00:02:12.577
Part 1.

23
00:02:12.857 --> 00:02:14.393
The System in One Page.

24
00:02:15.643 --> 00:02:17.542
What a hospital's software must do.

25
00:02:17.822 --> 00:02:23.326
A hospital is a 24 times 7 operation where information errors can harm people:

26
00:02:23.606 --> 00:02:26.443
A lab result that reaches the wrong patient's chart.

27
00:02:26.723 --> 00:02:30.201
A drug allergy that isn't visible when the prescription is written.

28
00:02:30.481 --> 00:02:32.230
A bed shown as free that isn't.

29
00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:37.395
A discharge delayed for hours because billing is waiting for an insurer's approval.

30
00:02:38.745 --> 00:02:52.292
The software's job is to make the right information available to the right person at the right moment, to record everything that was done (legally and clinically), and to coordinate hundreds of staff, rooms, machines and payers.

31
00:02:53.642 --> 00:02:54.773
The main systems.

32
00:02:55.053 --> 00:02:56.397
Here is a quick table:

33
00:02:56.677 --> 00:02:59.023
System, and Common name, and What it does.

34
00:02:59.303 --> 00:03:00.818
Patient administration.

35
00:03:01.098 --> 00:03:02.015
Common name:

36
00:03:02.295 --> 00:03:03.341
PAS or ADT.

37
00:03:03.621 --> 00:03:04.538
What it does:

38
00:03:04.818 --> 00:03:09.234
Registration, identity, admission, discharge, transfer, bed census.

39
00:03:09.514 --> 00:03:10.431
Scheduling.

40
00:03:10.711 --> 00:03:11.629
What it does:

41
00:03:11.909 --> 00:03:16.645
OPD appointments, doctor rosters, OT schedules, telemedicine slots.

42
00:03:16.925 --> 00:03:18.439
Electronic health record.

43
00:03:18.719 --> 00:03:19.637
Common name:

44
00:03:19.917 --> 00:03:20.962
EHR or EMR.

45
00:03:21.242 --> 00:03:22.159
What it does:

46
00:03:22.439 --> 00:03:27.709
Problems, allergies, medications, vitals, notes, orders, results, discharge summaries.

47
00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:30.229
Computerised provider order entry.

48
00:03:30.509 --> 00:03:31.426
Common name:

49
00:03:31.706 --> 00:03:32.538
CPOE.

50
00:03:32.818 --> 00:03:33.735
What it does:

51
00:03:34.015 --> 00:03:38.687
Doctors order labs, imaging, drugs, diets, procedures, with safety checks.

52
00:03:38.967 --> 00:03:40.738
Laboratory information system.

53
00:03:41.018 --> 00:03:41.935
Common name:

54
00:03:42.215 --> 00:03:42.941
LIS.

55
00:03:43.221 --> 00:03:44.138
What it does:

56
00:03:44.418 --> 00:03:49.282
Sample collection, barcode tracking, analyser interfaces, results, validation.

57
00:03:50.632 --> 00:03:53.811
Radiology information system + image archive.

58
00:03:54.091 --> 00:03:55.008
Common name:

59
00:03:55.288 --> 00:03:56.931
RIS or PACS or VNA.

60
00:03:57.211 --> 00:03:58.128
What it does:

61
00:03:58.408 --> 00:04:02.227
Imaging orders, modality worklists, DICOM images, reports.

62
00:04:02.507 --> 00:04:03.381
Pharmacy.

63
00:04:03.661 --> 00:04:04.579
What it does:

64
00:04:04.859 --> 00:04:07.909
Dispensing, formulary, stock, controlled drugs.

65
00:04:08.189 --> 00:04:10.557
Nursing and medication administration.

66
00:04:10.837 --> 00:04:12.309
Common name: eMAR.

67
00:04:12.589 --> 00:04:13.507
What it does:

68
00:04:13.787 --> 00:04:18.309
Care plans, vitals, bedside barcode-verified drug administration.

69
00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:20.275
Operating theatre management.

70
00:04:20.555 --> 00:04:21.472
Common name:

71
00:04:21.752 --> 00:04:22.413
OT.

72
00:04:22.693 --> 00:04:23.611
What it does:

73
00:04:23.891 --> 00:04:27.517
Scheduling, checklists, anaesthesia records, instrument tracking.

74
00:04:27.797 --> 00:04:29.227
Emergency department.

75
00:04:29.507 --> 00:04:30.424
Common name:

76
00:04:30.704 --> 00:04:31.344
ED.

77
00:04:31.624 --> 00:04:32.541
What it does:

78
00:04:32.821 --> 00:04:35.509
Triage, tracking board, fast registration.

79
00:04:36.859 --> 00:04:37.990
Revenue cycle.

80
00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:39.187
Common name:

81
00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:40.854
Billing or RCM.

82
00:04:41.134 --> 00:04:42.051
What it does:

83
00:04:42.331 --> 00:04:47.174
Charges, packages, deposits, insurance pre-authorisation, claims, payments.

84
00:04:47.454 --> 00:04:48.542
Supply chain.

85
00:04:48.822 --> 00:04:49.739
Common name:

86
00:04:50.019 --> 00:04:50.958
Inventory.

87
00:04:51.238 --> 00:04:52.155
What it does:

88
00:04:52.435 --> 00:04:56.147
Drugs, consumables, implants; purchase, stock, expiry.

89
00:04:56.427 --> 00:04:57.515
Telemedicine.

90
00:04:57.795 --> 00:04:58.713
What it does:

91
00:04:58.993 --> 00:05:02.470
Video consultations, e-prescriptions, remote follow-up.

92
00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:04.179
Patient app and portal.

93
00:05:04.459 --> 00:05:05.377
What it does:

94
00:05:05.657 --> 00:05:08.601
Booking, reports, bills, teleconsults, consent.

95
00:05:08.881 --> 00:05:10.225
Integration engine.

96
00:05:10.505 --> 00:05:11.422
What it does:

97
00:05:11.702 --> 00:05:16.545
HL7 v2 and FHIR messages, device feeds, ABDM, insurers.

98
00:05:16.825 --> 00:05:18.361
Clinical decision support.

99
00:05:18.641 --> 00:05:19.558
Common name:

100
00:05:19.838 --> 00:05:20.841
CDS.

101
00:05:21.121 --> 00:05:22.038
What it does:

102
00:05:22.318 --> 00:05:25.966
Drug checks, early-warning scores, sepsis alerts, reminders.

103
00:05:27.316 --> 00:05:28.511
Data platform.

104
00:05:28.791 --> 00:05:29.708
Common name:

105
00:05:29.988 --> 00:05:31.183
Lake or warehouse.

106
00:05:31.463 --> 00:05:32.380
What it does:

107
00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.369
Reporting, quality indicators, research, ML.

108
00:05:36.719 --> 00:05:38.874
Scale for this design (assumptions).

109
00:05:39.154 --> 00:05:40.498
Here is a quick table:

110
00:05:40.778 --> 00:05:42.101
Quantity, and Value.

111
00:05:42.381 --> 00:05:44.279
Hospitals or beds or clinics:

112
00:05:44.559 --> 00:05:46.437
10 or 3,000 or 30.

113
00:05:46.717 --> 00:05:53.138
OPD visits per day: ~15,000 (in person) + ~2,000 teleconsults.

114
00:05:53.418 --> 00:05:57.301
Admissions per day: ~600; average stay 4 days.

115
00:05:57.581 --> 00:06:00.823
ED visits per day: ~1,200.

116
00:06:01.103 --> 00:06:05.135
Lab tests per day: ~60,000 (many tests per sample).

117
00:06:05.415 --> 00:06:13.309
Imaging studies per day: ~3,000; CT or MRI studies are 100 MB, 2 GB each.

118
00:06:13.589 --> 00:06:18.133
Vitals observations: ~2M per day (monitors + nursing).

119
00:06:18.413 --> 00:06:23.191
Staff users: ~20,000; ~6,000 concurrent at peak.

120
00:06:24.541 --> 00:06:25.757
Records retention:

121
00:06:26.037 --> 00:06:30.773
Many years; imaging petabyte-scale over time (VERIFY retention rules).

122
00:06:32.123 --> 00:06:33.510
Estimation highlights:

123
00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:35.411
Imaging dominates storage.

124
00:06:35.691 --> 00:06:47.489
3,000 studies per day at an average of ~150 MB is ~450 GB per day, or ~160 TB per year before compression.

125
00:06:47.769 --> 00:06:50.030
Clinical data is small but critical.

126
00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:53.723
Notes, orders and results total tens of GB per day.

127
00:06:54.003 --> 00:06:55.134
Latency needs:

128
00:06:55.414 --> 00:06:59.681
Clinical screens: open a patient chart in 1 s at p95.

129
00:06:59.961 --> 00:07:01.369
Order entry with checks:

130
00:07:01.649 --> 00:07:02.502
2 s.

131
00:07:03.852 --> 00:07:06.839
Lab analyser interfaces: near real time.

132
00:07:07.119 --> 00:07:09.935
ED tracking board: updates within seconds.

133
00:07:11.285 --> 00:07:12.159
Part 2.

134
00:07:12.439 --> 00:07:13.399
Requirements.

135
00:07:14.649 --> 00:07:15.503
Functional.

136
00:07:15.783 --> 00:07:24.871
Register patients once and recognise them everywhere (one patient, one record), linking the national health ID (ABHA) with consent (VERIFY).

137
00:07:25.151 --> 00:07:31.892
Book, reschedule and cancel appointments across doctors, clinics and teleconsults, with queue tokens on the day.

138
00:07:32.172 --> 00:07:37.740
Triage and treat emergency patients with minimal registration first (register later).

139
00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:43.055
Admit, transfer and discharge patients; manage beds and housekeeping turnaround.

140
00:07:44.405 --> 00:07:53.962
Document care (history, examination, diagnoses, notes, vitals, nursing charts) and order tests, imaging, drugs and procedures, with safety checks.

141
00:07:54.242 --> 00:08:01.538
Run the lab from sample collection to validated results; the radiology workflow from order to report with images.

142
00:08:01.818 --> 00:08:05.573
Dispense and administer medicines with bedside verification.

143
00:08:05.853 --> 00:08:07.858
Schedule and document surgeries.

144
00:08:08.138 --> 00:08:15.925
Bill correctly (itemised and packages), take deposits, obtain insurance pre-authorisation, and submit and track claims.

145
00:08:17.275 --> 00:08:21.435
Offer teleconsultations with video, documents and e-prescriptions.

146
00:08:21.715 --> 00:08:27.752
Exchange records with other providers and national systems (FHIR, ABDM), with patient consent.

147
00:08:28.032 --> 00:08:34.304
Report quality indicators, statutory reports and management dashboards; support approved research.

148
00:08:35.654 --> 00:08:36.763
Non-functional.

149
00:08:37.043 --> 00:08:38.387
Here is a quick table:

150
00:08:38.667 --> 00:08:40.033
Attribute, and Target.

151
00:08:40.313 --> 00:08:41.379
Availability:

152
00:08:41.659 --> 00:08:51.963
Clinical core ≥ 99.95%, with downtime procedures (read-only copies, printed MARs) for anything below that.

153
00:08:52.243 --> 00:08:53.161
Latency:

154
00:08:53.441 --> 00:08:59.478
Chart open p95 1 s; result delivery to the chart 1 min after validation.

155
00:08:59.758 --> 00:09:00.633
Integrity:

156
00:09:00.913 --> 00:09:08.913
No result ever filed to the wrong patient; every clinical entry signed, timestamped and immutable (amendments are new versions).

157
00:09:09.193 --> 00:09:10.195
Security:

158
00:09:10.475 --> 00:09:16.342
Role-based and relationship-based access, break-glass with audit, encryption, device control.

159
00:09:16.622 --> 00:09:17.539
Privacy:

160
00:09:17.819 --> 00:09:23.110
Consent-based sharing; minimum necessary; DPDP Act obligations (VERIFY).

161
00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:24.371
Auditability:

162
00:09:24.651 --> 00:09:27.851
Who viewed or changed what, and when, for every record.

163
00:09:29.201 --> 00:09:30.460
Interoperability:

164
00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:36.564
HL7 v2, FHIR R4, DICOM, ABDM APIs (VERIFY versions).

165
00:09:36.844 --> 00:09:37.740
Recovery:

166
00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:47.492
RPO about 0 for clinical data in-region; RTO 1 h for the clinical core; imaging archive recoverable within a day for older studies.

167
00:09:48.842 --> 00:09:49.738
Part 3.

168
00:09:50.018 --> 00:09:51.533
High-Level Architecture.

169
00:09:52.783 --> 00:09:53.828
Component view.

170
00:09:54.108 --> 00:09:55.452
Here is the diagram.

171
00:09:59.302 --> 00:10:00.795
Architectural decisions.

172
00:10:01.075 --> 00:10:02.419
Here is a quick table:

173
00:10:02.699 --> 00:10:04.470
Decision, and Choice, and Why.

174
00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:06.478
Monolith versus services.

175
00:10:06.758 --> 00:10:07.590
Choice:

176
00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:20.179
A modular core (patient, EHR, orders, results in one strongly consistent domain) plus separate services for scheduling, billing, pharmacy stock, imaging, telemedicine and integration.

177
00:10:20.459 --> 00:10:21.142
Why:

178
00:10:21.422 --> 00:10:25.497
Clinical data needs transactions across orders, results and notes.

179
00:10:25.777 --> 00:10:30.214
Operational domains scale and change independently (Ch 34).

180
00:10:30.494 --> 00:10:31.774
Clinical data model.

181
00:10:32.054 --> 00:10:32.886
Choice:

182
00:10:33.166 --> 00:10:46.585
FHIR R4 resources as the canonical model (Patient, Encounter, Observation, MedicationRequest, ServiceRequest, DiagnosticReport, Condition, AllergyIntolerance, DocumentReference, Consent).

183
00:10:46.865 --> 00:10:47.547
Why:

184
00:10:47.827 --> 00:10:52.179
International standard, used by ABDM for record exchange (VERIFY).

185
00:10:52.459 --> 00:10:55.979
It avoids a custom model that needs translation everywhere.

186
00:10:57.329 --> 00:10:58.993
Storage of clinical data.

187
00:10:59.273 --> 00:11:00.105
Choice:

188
00:11:00.385 --> 00:11:08.321
PostgreSQL behind a FHIR server (resources as versioned JSONB plus search indexes), with append-only history.

189
00:11:08.601 --> 00:11:09.284
Why:

190
00:11:09.564 --> 00:11:14.428
Transactions, versioning, and SQL for operations (Ch 05).

191
00:11:14.708 --> 00:11:15.711
Integration.

192
00:11:15.991 --> 00:11:16.823
Choice:

193
00:11:17.103 --> 00:11:25.956
An integration engine for HL7 v2 (lab analysers, legacy systems) and FHIR, plus an event bus for internal events.

194
00:11:26.236 --> 00:11:26.919
Why:

195
00:11:27.199 --> 00:11:32.276
Hospitals are full of devices and vendor systems that speak HL7 v2.

196
00:11:32.556 --> 00:11:33.388
Images.

197
00:11:33.668 --> 00:11:34.500
Choice:

198
00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:38.193
PACS or VNA with DICOM, on tiered object storage.

199
00:11:38.473 --> 00:11:39.156
Why:

200
00:11:39.436 --> 00:11:42.679
Images are huge and have their own standard and viewers.

201
00:11:42.959 --> 00:11:43.876
Deployment.

202
00:11:44.156 --> 00:11:44.988
Choice:

203
00:11:45.268 --> 00:11:53.823
Private cloud or on-premises Kubernetes in each region, with a DR site; edge nodes in each hospital for downtime resilience.

204
00:11:54.103 --> 00:11:54.785
Why:

205
00:11:55.065 --> 00:12:00.847
Clinical systems must keep working when the WAN link fails; data residency (VERIFY).

206
00:12:02.197 --> 00:12:03.114
Part 4.

207
00:12:03.394 --> 00:12:05.698
How It Works, Patient Journeys End to End.

208
00:12:06.948 --> 00:12:09.657
Journey 1: an OPD visit booked in the app.

209
00:12:09.937 --> 00:12:11.068
Search and booking.

210
00:12:11.348 --> 00:12:15.124
The patient searches by specialty, doctor or symptoms in the app.

211
00:12:15.404 --> 00:12:21.463
Search finds doctors (OpenSearch index of doctors, specialties, languages and locations).

212
00:12:21.743 --> 00:12:23.919
The scheduling service shows free slots.

213
00:12:24.199 --> 00:12:41.351
Slots come from each doctor's roster templates (for example, Mon or Wed or Fri 10:00 to 13:00, 12-minute slots), minus leaves and blocks, plus overbooking allowance computed from predicted no-shows (§6.3).

214
00:12:42.701 --> 00:12:43.874
Holding the slot.

215
00:12:44.154 --> 00:12:51.322
The app holds the slot for 5 minutes (a hold record with a TTL), collects the fee if prepaid, and confirms.

216
00:12:51.602 --> 00:13:02.546
Double booking is prevented by a unique constraint on (doctor, slot, sequence) and a conditional insert, exactly like inventory reservation (Ch 46).

217
00:13:02.826 --> 00:13:03.743
Identity.

218
00:13:04.023 --> 00:13:14.029
At first booking, the patient is created or matched in the Master Patient Index (MPI) using name, date of birth, gender, mobile and ABHA number if given.

219
00:13:14.309 --> 00:13:17.637
The MPI returns a medical record number (MRN).

220
00:13:18.987 --> 00:13:26.389
If a probable duplicate exists, the record is flagged for review rather than merged automatically (§6.1).

221
00:13:26.669 --> 00:13:27.629
Reminders.

222
00:13:27.909 --> 00:13:38.299
SMS or WhatsApp or push reminders go out 24 h and 2 h before (Ch 40), with a one-tap cancel that frees the slot for waitlisted patients.

223
00:13:38.579 --> 00:13:39.432
Arrival.

224
00:13:39.712 --> 00:13:42.933
The patient scans a QR at a kiosk or the front desk.

225
00:13:43.213 --> 00:13:47.352
The queue service issues a token and puts the patient in the doctor's queue.

226
00:13:48.702 --> 00:13:57.790
The queue order considers appointment time, arrival time and priority (elderly, pregnant, follow-up-with-reports), shown on waiting-room screens.

227
00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:02.337
Expected wait is computed from the doctor's recent consultation times.

228
00:14:02.617 --> 00:14:03.918
Pre-consultation.

229
00:14:04.198 --> 00:14:06.374
A nurse records vitals on a tablet.

230
00:14:06.654 --> 00:14:10.729
The vitals become FHIR Observations linked to today's Encounter.

231
00:14:11.009 --> 00:14:12.054
Consultation.

232
00:14:12.334 --> 00:14:13.955
The doctor opens the chart:

233
00:14:15.305 --> 00:14:20.127
Summary: problems, allergies, current medications, recent results, past visits.

234
00:14:20.407 --> 00:14:34.380
Documentation: the doctor writes a note (templates per specialty, voice dictation optional) and records diagnoses (coded, for example, ICD-10, with SNOMED CT where used; VERIFY coding standards adopted).

235
00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:37.881
Orders: labs, imaging and prescriptions, through CPOE.

236
00:14:38.161 --> 00:14:44.753
Safety checks at order time (CDS): drug, allergy, drug, drug interaction, duplicate therapy;.

237
00:14:46.103 --> 00:14:54.295
dose range for age and weight, and renal adjustment if recent creatinine is available;. duplicate test within a time window.

238
00:14:54.575 --> 00:15:00.293
Alerts are tiered (hard stop versus warning with override reason), to avoid alert fatigue.

239
00:15:00.573 --> 00:15:01.469
Checkout.

240
00:15:01.749 --> 00:15:08.575
The billing service turns orders into charges using the tariff master (item prices by payer, package rules).

241
00:15:09.925 --> 00:15:15.152
The patient pays at the counter or in the app (UPI or card; Ch 45).

242
00:15:15.432 --> 00:15:19.059
The e-prescription is sent to the pharmacy and to the patient's app.

243
00:15:19.339 --> 00:15:25.269
A visit summary is shared, and pushed to the patient's ABDM-linked record if consented (§8).

244
00:15:25.549 --> 00:15:26.765
Sample collection.

245
00:15:27.045 --> 00:15:29.499
Lab orders appear in the phlebotomy worklist.

246
00:15:29.779 --> 00:15:34.771
Barcoded labels are printed per sample container, and the collection time is scanned.

247
00:15:35.051 --> 00:15:35.925
Results.

248
00:15:37.275 --> 00:15:49.094
Analysers send results over HL7 v2 or ASTM to the LIS, which applies auto-verification rules: delta checks, reference ranges, instrument flags.

249
00:15:49.374 --> 00:15:51.742
A pathologist validates the exceptions.

250
00:15:52.022 --> 00:15:57.355
Validated results post to the EHR as Observations and DiagnosticReports.

251
00:15:57.635 --> 00:15:59.769
The doctor and patient are notified.

252
00:16:00.049 --> 00:16:04.230
Critical values trigger a phone call workflow with acknowledgement tracking.

253
00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:09.228
Journey 2: an emergency patient who becomes an in-patient.

254
00:16:09.508 --> 00:16:10.361
Arrival.

255
00:16:10.641 --> 00:16:15.911
The patient arrives by ambulance, and pre-arrival details may come from the ambulance app.

256
00:16:16.191 --> 00:16:22.164
Quick registration creates an unknown or minimal patient (temporary MRN) in seconds.

257
00:16:22.444 --> 00:16:26.412
Identity is reconciled later by the MPI's merge workflow.

258
00:16:26.692 --> 00:16:27.545
Triage.

259
00:16:27.825 --> 00:16:36.679
A nurse assigns a triage level, for example, a 5-level scale such as ESI, per hospital protocol (VERIFY which scale is used).

260
00:16:36.959 --> 00:16:43.529
The ED tracking board shows every patient's location, level, waiting time, pending orders and results.

261
00:16:44.879 --> 00:16:47.717
Updates are pushed over WebSockets from ED events.

262
00:16:47.997 --> 00:16:48.999
Early warning.

263
00:16:49.279 --> 00:16:54.741
Vitals from monitors reach the device gateway, which converts them to FHIR Observations.

264
00:16:55.021 --> 00:17:01.997
An early-warning score (for example, NEWS2; §6.4) is computed on every new vital set.

265
00:17:02.277 --> 00:17:06.799
A rising score pages the responsible doctor through escalation rules.

266
00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:11.154
Stat orders go to the lab and radiology with a priority flag.

267
00:17:12.504 --> 00:17:22.168
Radiology's modality worklist (DICOM MWL) sends the patient's details to the CT scanner, so the images carry the correct identifiers.

268
00:17:22.448 --> 00:17:26.629
Images go to PACS, and the radiologist reports in the RIS.

269
00:17:26.909 --> 00:17:31.453
The report posts to the EHR, and a link opens the images in a web viewer.

270
00:17:31.733 --> 00:17:33.013
Decision to admit.

271
00:17:33.293 --> 00:17:43.917
A bed request is created with the needs: specialty and level (ward or HDU or ICU);. isolation, gender and equipment;. near nursing station or not.

272
00:17:45.267 --> 00:17:46.313
Bed assignment.

273
00:17:46.593 --> 00:17:57.899
The bed management service proposes beds using a constraint-based assignment (§6.2), considering beds that will be free after pending discharges and housekeeping.

274
00:17:58.179 --> 00:17:59.801
The bed manager confirms.

275
00:18:00.081 --> 00:18:03.131
ADT records the admission and transfer events.

276
00:18:03.411 --> 00:18:06.206
Housekeeping and transport tasks are generated.

277
00:18:06.486 --> 00:18:07.553
In-patient care:

278
00:18:07.833 --> 00:18:13.401
Admission orders, often from an order set, for example, community-acquired pneumonia adult.

279
00:18:14.751 --> 00:18:20.809
Medication orders: the pharmacist verifies them (clinical check), then dispenses (unit dose).

280
00:18:21.089 --> 00:18:26.060
Bedside administration: nurses scan the patient wristband and the drug barcode.

281
00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:33.124
The eMAR checks the five rights (patient, drug, dose, route, time) before recording administration.

282
00:18:33.404 --> 00:18:36.924
Nursing: documentation, care plans and fluid balance.

283
00:18:37.204 --> 00:18:38.804
Doctors: progress notes.

284
00:18:40.154 --> 00:18:41.477
Billing during the stay.

285
00:18:41.757 --> 00:18:50.653
Charges accumulate on the in-patient account (room rent per day by room category, drugs, consumables, tests, procedures, doctor visits).

286
00:18:50.933 --> 00:18:59.957
If insured, a pre-authorisation request with diagnosis and estimated cost goes to the insurer or TPA (§4.4).

287
00:19:00.237 --> 00:19:02.498
Deposits are tracked against the running bill.

288
00:19:02.778 --> 00:19:03.781
Discharge.

289
00:19:05.131 --> 00:19:11.339
The doctor writes the discharge summary: diagnoses, procedures, course, medications, follow-up.

290
00:19:11.619 --> 00:19:15.800
Pharmacy reconciles medications, and nursing completes the checklist.

291
00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:20.731
Billing produces the final bill, and the insurer approves the final claim amount.

292
00:19:21.011 --> 00:19:22.248
The patient leaves.

293
00:19:22.528 --> 00:19:25.493
The bed goes to housekeeping, then back to available.

294
00:19:25.773 --> 00:19:28.355
The discharge event updates bed forecasts.

295
00:19:29.705 --> 00:19:37.235
Discharge delay (from medically fit to left the bed) is a key metric, often dominated by billing and insurance steps.

296
00:19:38.585 --> 00:19:40.569
Journey 3: a planned surgery.

297
00:19:40.849 --> 00:19:41.895
Booking the case.

298
00:19:42.175 --> 00:19:49.471
The surgeon books the case with procedure, duration estimate, surgeon, anaesthesia type, implants and special equipment.

299
00:19:49.751 --> 00:19:57.111
Pre-operative assessment: tests, anaesthesia assessment, consent forms (e-signed), and pre-authorisation if insured.

300
00:19:57.391 --> 00:20:09.081
OT scheduling assigns theatre, date and sequence (§6.5), checking the constraints: surgeon availability;. equipment conflicts (one C-arm shared by two theatres);.

301
00:20:10.431 --> 00:20:13.333
implant availability, reserved in inventory;.

302
00:20:13.613 --> 00:20:16.045
ICU bed after surgery, if needed.

303
00:20:16.325 --> 00:20:17.199
On the day:

304
00:20:17.479 --> 00:20:23.282
The surgical safety checklist (sign-in, time-out, sign-out) is recorded in the OT app.

305
00:20:23.562 --> 00:20:26.079
Anaesthesia records capture device data.

306
00:20:26.359 --> 00:20:32.887
Implants are scanned (lot and serial for traceability), and instruments are tracked through sterilisation cycles.

307
00:20:34.237 --> 00:20:40.659
After surgery: the recovery room, then the ward or ICU, with the transfer recorded in ADT.

308
00:20:40.939 --> 00:20:46.613
The billing package is applied (for example, a fixed-price package with inclusions and exclusions).

309
00:20:47.963 --> 00:20:50.054
Journey 4: cashless insurance.

310
00:20:50.334 --> 00:20:57.331
At admission, the insurance desk verifies the policy (payer API or portal) and captures eligibility.

311
00:20:57.611 --> 00:21:05.825
Pre-authorisation request: diagnosis codes, planned procedure, estimated cost, clinical notes and investigation reports.

312
00:21:06.105 --> 00:21:16.259
It goes by the payer's API or portal, or through the National Health Claims Exchange (NHCX, FHIR-based) where adopted (VERIFY status and scope).

313
00:21:16.539 --> 00:21:20.635
The payer approves an amount, queries for more information, or rejects.

314
00:21:20.915 --> 00:21:24.329
Queries become tasks for doctors and the insurance desk.

315
00:21:24.609 --> 00:21:27.787
Response time is tracked, because it delays discharge.

316
00:21:29.137 --> 00:21:31.868
Enhancements are requested if the stay extends.

317
00:21:32.148 --> 00:21:37.012
Final bill: the claim is submitted with the itemised bill and discharge summary.

318
00:21:37.292 --> 00:21:42.412
The payer deducts non-payable items (per policy), and the patient pays the difference.

319
00:21:42.692 --> 00:21:46.532
Payment reconciliation: payer remittances are matched to claims.

320
00:21:46.812 --> 00:21:52.508
Deductions are analysed by reason (§6.7) to reduce future denials.

321
00:21:53.858 --> 00:21:56.034
Journey 5: a teleconsultation.

322
00:21:56.314 --> 00:22:03.546
Booking and payment: the patient books a teleconsult slot (same scheduling engine, channel = video) and pays.

323
00:22:03.826 --> 00:22:04.615
Joining:

324
00:22:04.895 --> 00:22:08.543
10 minutes before, the patient joins a virtual waiting room.

325
00:22:08.823 --> 00:22:11.554
The app checks camera, microphone and bandwidth.

326
00:22:11.834 --> 00:22:17.061
The call: the doctor starts the call from the clinician workspace, with the chart on one side.

327
00:22:17.341 --> 00:22:31.399
Video uses WebRTC: clients connect to a selective forwarding unit (SFU), through TURN relays when networks block peer connections;. bandwidth adapts through simulcast or SVC and congestion control.

328
00:22:32.749 --> 00:22:36.653
On poor networks the call falls back to audio-only, then to chat.

329
00:22:36.933 --> 00:22:41.221
Documentation: the doctor documents, prescribes and orders tests.

330
00:22:41.501 --> 00:22:55.091
The e-prescription follows the Telemedicine Practice Guidelines (VERIFY): doctor registration number, restrictions on which drug categories can be prescribed remotely, and the patient's identity and consent recorded.

331
00:22:55.371 --> 00:23:00.811
After the call: the prescription goes to the patient's app and optionally to a partner pharmacy.

332
00:23:01.091 --> 00:23:03.416
Test orders create home-collection requests.

333
00:23:03.696 --> 00:23:05.275
A follow-up is scheduled.

334
00:23:06.625 --> 00:23:09.611
Recording: calls are normally not stored as video.

335
00:23:09.891 --> 00:23:16.825
The clinical note is the record, and any recording requires explicit consent and a retention policy (VERIFY).

336
00:23:18.175 --> 00:23:21.545
Journey 6: sharing records via ABDM (consent-based).

337
00:23:21.825 --> 00:23:31.191
Linking: the patient links their ABHA number to the hospital's MRN, which makes the hospital a Health Information Provider (HIP) for that patient.

338
00:23:31.471 --> 00:23:36.612
Request: another provider, a Health Information User (HIU), requests records.

339
00:23:36.892 --> 00:23:45.105
The patient's consent manager app asks the patient to approve a consent artefact: purpose, date range, record types and expiry.

340
00:23:45.385 --> 00:23:49.908
Delivery: on approval, the hospital's ABDM gateway receives the request.

341
00:23:50.188 --> 00:23:57.164
It fetches the matching records (FHIR bundles), encrypts them for the requester, and sends them over the ABDM data flow.

342
00:23:57.444 --> 00:24:00.260
It logs everything against the consent artefact.

343
00:24:01.610 --> 00:24:11.082
The hospital never sends data without a valid consent artefact, and revocations stop further sharing (VERIFY exact protocol and specifications).

344
00:24:12.432 --> 00:24:13.392
Part 5.

345
00:24:13.672 --> 00:24:15.208
Core Subsystems in Depth.

346
00:24:16.458 --> 00:24:18.677
Master Patient Index (MPI).

347
00:24:18.957 --> 00:24:23.351
Purpose: one person, one MRN across all hospitals and clinics.

348
00:24:23.631 --> 00:24:26.639
Duplicates cause missed allergies and split histories.

349
00:24:26.919 --> 00:24:30.418
Wrong merges are worse, because they mix two people's records.

350
00:24:30.698 --> 00:24:42.879
Data: demographics and identifiers (MRN per facility, enterprise ID, ABHA number, mobile, government IDs only where lawful);. addresses and aliases;. a match history.

351
00:24:44.229 --> 00:24:54.725
Matching (§6.1) is probabilistic: auto-link above an upper threshold;. queue for human review in the grey zone;. new record below the lower threshold.

352
00:24:55.005 --> 00:24:58.867
Merge and unmerge are first-class operations with full audit.

353
00:24:59.147 --> 00:25:04.779
Downstream systems receive merge events and repoint their references (a FHIR Patient.link).

354
00:25:06.129 --> 00:25:08.497
EHR and the clinical data store.

355
00:25:08.777 --> 00:25:09.523
Model:

356
00:25:09.803 --> 00:25:10.977
FHIR resources.

357
00:25:11.257 --> 00:25:12.217
The main ones:

358
00:25:12.497 --> 00:25:15.889
Patient, Encounter (visit or admission), EpisodeOfCare;.

359
00:25:16.169 --> 00:25:18.110
Condition, AllergyIntolerance;.

360
00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:21.825
Observation (vitals, labs), DiagnosticReport;.

361
00:25:22.105 --> 00:25:25.113
MedicationRequest, MedicationAdministration;.

362
00:25:25.393 --> 00:25:27.697
ServiceRequest (orders), Procedure;.

363
00:25:27.977 --> 00:25:31.177
DocumentReference (notes, scanned documents), Consent.

364
00:25:32.527 --> 00:25:33.423
Storage:

365
00:25:33.703 --> 00:25:42.364
Each resource type is stored as versioned JSONB plus search parameter tables (patient, date, code, status) in PostgreSQL.

366
00:25:42.644 --> 00:25:47.060
A FHIR server provides this (for example, HAPI FHIR JPA).

367
00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:50.903
Every update creates a new version, and the history is immutable.

368
00:25:51.183 --> 00:25:56.260
Amendments to signed notes are new versions with a reason, and the original remains visible.

369
00:25:56.540 --> 00:26:05.564
The chart view is assembled by a read model, a per-patient summary projection (latest vitals, active problems, active meds, recent results):

370
00:26:06.914 --> 00:26:16.791
it is updated from events, so opening a chart is one indexed read, not dozens of queries (Ch 15);. the source of truth remains the resource store.

371
00:26:17.071 --> 00:26:18.373
Clinical documents:

372
00:26:18.653 --> 00:26:20.637
Structured templates plus free text.

373
00:26:20.917 --> 00:26:29.941
Scanned paper and external PDFs are stored in object storage, referenced by DocumentReference, and made searchable after OCR where useful.

374
00:26:30.221 --> 00:26:31.330
Terminologies:

375
00:26:32.680 --> 00:26:48.467
A terminology service (for example, Snowstorm for SNOMED CT, plus LOINC for lab tests, ICD-10 for diagnoses and billing, and a local drug master mapped to a national list where available; VERIFY licensing and national standards).

376
00:26:48.747 --> 00:26:52.309
Codes make CDS rules, analytics and exchange possible.

377
00:26:53.659 --> 00:26:56.433
Orders (CPOE) and clinical decision support.

378
00:26:56.713 --> 00:27:06.334
Order lifecycle: draft, then signed, then (pharmacist or radiologist verification), then scheduled, then in progress, then completed or cancelled.

379
00:27:06.614 --> 00:27:10.497
Order sets bundle evidence-based orders for common conditions.

380
00:27:10.777 --> 00:27:11.950
CDS hooks:

381
00:27:12.230 --> 00:27:22.214
At order-select and order-sign, CDS services evaluate: rules on the patient context (allergies, current meds, labs, weight, age, pregnancy);.

382
00:27:23.564 --> 00:27:30.924
knowledge bases for interactions and dose ranges (commercial or national drug knowledge base; VERIFY licensing).

383
00:27:31.204 --> 00:27:36.196
The response is cards: info, warning (override with reason), or hard stop.

384
00:27:36.476 --> 00:27:45.799
Alert fatigue control: measure the override rate per rule, tune or retire noisy rules, and show only the highest-severity interaction per pair.

385
00:27:46.079 --> 00:27:53.652
Asynchronous CDS: early-warning scores and sepsis screening run on the observation stream (§6.4);.

386
00:27:55.002 --> 00:28:00.271
care-gap reminders (for example, diabetic foot exam overdue) run on schedules.

387
00:28:01.621 --> 00:28:03.285
Laboratory (LIS).

388
00:28:03.565 --> 00:28:04.397
Workflow:

389
00:28:04.677 --> 00:28:06.363
Order, then sample planning.

390
00:28:06.643 --> 00:28:09.203
Tests are grouped by container and sample type.

391
00:28:09.483 --> 00:28:14.261
Labelling and collection: barcode labels are printed and the collection is scanned.

392
00:28:14.541 --> 00:28:16.461
Transport and receipt at the lab.

393
00:28:16.741 --> 00:28:19.813
Analysis: samples are loaded onto analysers.

394
00:28:20.093 --> 00:28:25.725
Instruments query the LIS for the tests per barcode (host query), then return results.

395
00:28:27.075 --> 00:28:33.219
Auto-verification (§6.6) releases normal, consistent results automatically.

396
00:28:33.499 --> 00:28:36.913
The rest wait for technologist or pathologist review.

397
00:28:37.193 --> 00:28:42.889
Reporting: the report posts to the EHR, and critical values trigger phone-call workflows.

398
00:28:43.169 --> 00:28:44.662
Instrument interfaces:

399
00:28:44.942 --> 00:28:52.366
HL7 v2 or ASTM or LIS2 over TCP or serial through the integration engine.

400
00:28:52.646 --> 00:28:53.734
Quality control:

401
00:28:54.014 --> 00:28:55.678
QC runs are recorded.

402
00:28:55.958 --> 00:29:02.486
Levey, Jennings charts and Westgard rules detect analyser drift, and results are blocked if QC fails.

403
00:29:03.836 --> 00:29:11.708
Sample tracking: every scan (collected, received, loaded, stored) is an event, so turnaround time can be measured per step.

404
00:29:13.058 --> 00:29:15.426
Radiology (RIS or PACS).

405
00:29:15.706 --> 00:29:31.386
Order to scan: the RIS schedules the modality slot;. the DICOM Modality Worklist gives the scanner patient and accession details;. images go to PACS via DICOM C-STORE (or DICOMweb STOW-RS);. the RIS tracks status.

406
00:29:31.666 --> 00:29:32.562
Storage:

407
00:29:33.912 --> 00:29:47.651
tiered: recent studies on fast storage for reading, older ones compressed (lossless) in object storage (a vendor-neutral archive), with lifecycle rules;. pre-fetching of prior studies when a patient is scheduled.

408
00:29:47.931 --> 00:30:00.176
Viewing: radiologists use diagnostic workstations;. clinicians use a zero-footprint web viewer over DICOMweb (WADO-RS), streaming the frames needed rather than the whole study.

409
00:30:01.526 --> 00:30:12.001
Reporting: structured templates and voice dictation;. reports are signed and posted as DiagnosticReports;. critical findings follow a communication workflow.

410
00:30:12.281 --> 00:30:20.238
AI assist (optional): triage models, such as intracranial haemorrhage detection on CT, prioritise the worklist.

411
00:30:20.518 --> 00:30:27.451
They are regulated as medical devices where applicable (VERIFY), and always sit under radiologist review.

412
00:30:28.801 --> 00:30:31.255
Pharmacy and medication administration.

413
00:30:31.535 --> 00:30:37.849
Formulary and drug master: brands mapped to generics, strengths, forms, prices and stock units.

414
00:30:38.129 --> 00:30:39.239
In-patient flow:

415
00:30:39.519 --> 00:30:40.244
Order.

416
00:30:40.524 --> 00:30:45.324
Pharmacist verification: clinical checks, substitutions per formulary.

417
00:30:45.604 --> 00:30:54.265
Dispensing: unit doses per ward per round, often by cart cycles. eMAR schedule is generated from the order frequency.

418
00:30:54.545 --> 00:30:56.935
Barcode administration at the bedside.

419
00:30:58.285 --> 00:31:03.554
Controlled drugs: double sign-off, a register, and stock reconciliation per shift.

420
00:31:03.834 --> 00:31:05.946
Stock: batch and expiry tracking.

421
00:31:06.226 --> 00:31:08.871
FEFO (first-expiry-first-out) picking.

422
00:31:09.151 --> 00:31:11.477
Reorder points (§6.8).

423
00:31:11.757 --> 00:31:13.058
Returns from wards.

424
00:31:14.408 --> 00:31:16.456
Beds, ADT and patient flow.

425
00:31:16.736 --> 00:31:24.715
ADT events are the heartbeat of the hospital: admit, transfer, discharge, leave of absence, and cancel versions of each.

426
00:31:24.995 --> 00:31:32.120
They are published on the event bus so lab, pharmacy, diet, billing and housekeeping follow the patient automatically.

427
00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:39.355
Bed states: available, then reserved, then occupied, then dirty (after discharge), then cleaning, then available.

428
00:31:39.635 --> 00:31:42.835
Blocked (maintenance or isolation) is a separate state.

429
00:31:43.115 --> 00:31:54.891
Patient-flow command centre: live census, expected discharges today, ED boarders, OT schedule, ICU capacity, and the bed demand forecast (§6.2).

430
00:31:56.241 --> 00:31:58.523
Revenue cycle (billing and insurance).

431
00:31:58.803 --> 00:32:06.355
Charge capture: every billable event (room day, order completed, drug dispensed, implant used) emits a charge event.

432
00:32:06.635 --> 00:32:18.518
The billing service prices it with the tariff master, which holds: price by payer contract (cash, corporate, insurer, government scheme);. package definitions (inclusions, exclusions, caps).

433
00:32:18.798 --> 00:32:25.369
Package logic: a surgery package may include room stay up to N days, standard drugs and consumables.

434
00:32:25.649 --> 00:32:31.387
Items beyond it are billed separately, and the engine decides per charge line whether it is included.

435
00:32:32.737 --> 00:32:40.396
Deposits, refunds and payment modes, using Ch 45 patterns (idempotent payments, ledger, reconciliation).

436
00:32:40.676 --> 00:32:44.964
Claims: pre-auth, enhancement and final claim, with document bundles.

437
00:32:45.244 --> 00:32:47.868
The claim status is tracked per payer SLA.

438
00:32:48.148 --> 00:32:51.988
Denial analytics find root causes (§6.7).

439
00:32:52.268 --> 00:33:01.527
Accounting: posting to the finance system (GL) with GST where applicable (VERIFY GST treatment of healthcare services and items).

440
00:33:02.877 --> 00:33:04.327
Telemedicine platform.

441
00:33:04.607 --> 00:33:05.951
Here is a quick table:

442
00:33:06.231 --> 00:33:07.575
Component, and Role.

443
00:33:07.855 --> 00:33:09.071
Signalling service:

444
00:33:09.351 --> 00:33:12.786
WebSocket-based room management, join tokens, presence.

445
00:33:13.066 --> 00:33:14.239
SFU cluster:

446
00:33:14.519 --> 00:33:15.991
Forwards media streams.

447
00:33:16.271 --> 00:33:21.071
Per call, 2 to 4 participants (patient, doctor, interpreter or family).

448
00:33:21.351 --> 00:33:22.397
TURN servers:

449
00:33:22.677 --> 00:33:26.111
Relay for NAT or firewall traversal, in-region.

450
00:33:26.391 --> 00:33:27.842
Media quality service:

451
00:33:28.122 --> 00:33:34.373
Collects WebRTC stats (packet loss, jitter, bitrate) for troubleshooting and quality dashboards.

452
00:33:34.653 --> 00:33:35.634
Waiting room:

453
00:33:35.914 --> 00:33:38.410
Patient queue per doctor, with device checks.

454
00:33:38.690 --> 00:33:39.927
Clinical workspace:

455
00:33:40.207 --> 00:33:43.429
Chart, notes, e-prescription, orders beside the video.

456
00:33:44.779 --> 00:33:45.717
Capacity:

457
00:33:45.997 --> 00:33:52.547
2,000 teleconsults per day at 12 minutes each, with a peak of ~250 concurrent calls.

458
00:33:52.827 --> 00:33:58.800
A single SFU node handles hundreds of streams, so a small, regional cluster suffices.

459
00:33:59.080 --> 00:33:59.997
Privacy:

460
00:34:00.277 --> 00:34:05.312
Media is encrypted (DTLS-SRTP), and no recording by default.

461
00:34:05.592 --> 00:34:07.725
Identity is verified at booking.

462
00:34:08.005 --> 00:34:12.208
Consent is recorded in the encounter (VERIFY guideline requirements).

463
00:34:13.558 --> 00:34:14.902
Integration engine.

464
00:34:15.182 --> 00:34:20.537
Purpose: translate and route messages between systems that were never designed together.

465
00:34:20.817 --> 00:34:32.870
Inbound: lab analysers (HL7 v2 ORU, ASTM);. bedside monitors, via a device gateway to Observations;. external labs;. insurer responses;.

466
00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:34.323
ABDM requests.

467
00:34:35.673 --> 00:34:40.900
Outbound: orders to external labs (HL7 v2 ORM or OML);.

468
00:34:41.180 --> 00:34:50.353
ADT feeds to departmental systems (A01 admit, A02 transfer, A03 discharge, A08 update);.

469
00:34:50.633 --> 00:34:52.511
FHIR APIs for partners.

470
00:34:52.791 --> 00:35:02.327
Features: message transformation, code mapping, retries with dead-letter queues, replay, message tracking UI, and alerts on interface backlog.

471
00:35:02.607 --> 00:35:08.687
It is the most common source of silent failures, so every interface has a heartbeat and a backlog alarm.

472
00:35:10.037 --> 00:35:10.975
Part 6.

473
00:35:11.255 --> 00:35:12.471
Algorithms Used.

474
00:35:13.721 --> 00:35:17.412
Patient matching (MPI), probabilistic record linkage.

475
00:35:17.692 --> 00:35:20.508
Blocking: don't compare every record with every other.

476
00:35:20.788 --> 00:35:30.495
Compare only records sharing a blocking key, such as the first letters of the phonetically encoded surname plus birth year, the mobile number, or the ABHA number.

477
00:35:30.775 --> 00:35:33.527
This cuts comparisons from billions to thousands.

478
00:35:33.807 --> 00:35:52.281
Field comparisons: names with Jaro, Winkler similarity and phonetic codes (Double Metaphone), with Indian-name normalisation: initials, spelling variants, surname and given-name order, honorifics;. date of birth: exact, transposed day per month, or year ±1;.

479
00:35:53.631 --> 00:35:56.383
gender, mobile (exact), and address tokens.

480
00:35:56.663 --> 00:35:58.818
Scoring: the Fellegi, Sunter model.

481
00:35:59.098 --> 00:36:15.717
Each field agreement adds log(m or u) and each disagreement adds log((1minus m)/(1minus u)), where: m = the probability that the fields agree if the records are the same person;. u = the probability that they agree by chance.

482
00:36:15.997 --> 00:36:19.346
The weights are estimated from reviewed pairs (or with EM).

483
00:36:20.696 --> 00:36:26.243
Thresholds: auto-link above the upper threshold, human review in between, new record below.

484
00:36:26.523 --> 00:36:31.963
The thresholds are chosen by the cost of errors: a false merge is far worse than a false split.

485
00:36:32.243 --> 00:36:38.664
Monitoring: the duplicate rate, the review queue size, and unmerges (each one is a near-miss safety event).

486
00:36:40.014 --> 00:36:42.297
Bed assignment and demand forecasting.

487
00:36:42.577 --> 00:36:45.990
Assignment is a constraint-satisfaction and scoring problem.

488
00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:51.539
Hard constraints: gender (for shared rooms), isolation, level of care, equipment.

489
00:36:51.819 --> 00:37:01.846
Soft preferences: ward specialty match, proximity to the nursing station for high-risk patients, keeping teams' patients together, and minimising transfers.

490
00:37:02.126 --> 00:37:05.667
Method: score every feasible bed and propose the top 3.

491
00:37:05.947 --> 00:37:13.777
For batch assignment (morning admissions), solve as a weighted bipartite matching (Hungarian algorithm or min-cost flow).

492
00:37:15.127 --> 00:37:37.719
Forecasting: admissions by hour and day of week (seasonal time series such as SARIMA or Prophet or gradient-boosted models with calendar and epidemic features);. discharges from predicted length of stay per patient (a regression on diagnosis, age and procedures);. this yields expected free beds per ward over the next 24 to 72 h.

493
00:37:39.069 --> 00:37:40.029
Queueing view:

494
00:37:40.309 --> 00:37:47.626
ED boarding occurs when bed arrival rate times length of stay approaches capacity (Little's Law, Ch 31).

495
00:37:47.906 --> 00:37:56.205
Occupancy beyond ~85 to 90% makes waits explode (a common planning heuristic; VERIFY for your context).

496
00:37:57.555 --> 00:37:59.667
Appointment scheduling and overbooking.

497
00:37:59.947 --> 00:38:03.915
Slot generation from roster templates, minus leaves and blocks.

498
00:38:04.195 --> 00:38:13.901
No-show prediction: a logistic regression or gradient-boosted model on lead time, past no-shows, distance, weather, day and time, and payment status.

499
00:38:14.181 --> 00:38:19.621
Overbooking: add extra bookings per session so that expected attendance about capacity.

500
00:38:19.901 --> 00:38:28.499
Two limits: the expected overtime and waiting time must stay within policy;. no overbooking for procedures that need fixed resources.

501
00:38:29.849 --> 00:38:35.715
Queue ordering on the day: a priority queue by (priority class, appointment time, arrival time).

502
00:38:35.995 --> 00:38:43.142
Expected wait = queue position times a rolling average consultation time (by doctor and visit type).

503
00:38:44.492 --> 00:38:46.839
Early warning scores and sepsis screening.

504
00:38:47.119 --> 00:39:04.335
NEWS2-style scoring: points for respiratory rate, oxygen saturation, supplemental oxygen, systolic blood pressure, pulse, consciousness and temperature;. the total maps to the response level (routine, increased frequency, urgent review, emergency response).

505
00:39:04.615 --> 00:39:09.820
The scoring table and responses must follow the clinically approved protocol (VERIFY).

506
00:39:10.100 --> 00:39:17.012
Implementation: a stream processor consumes vital-sign Observations and recomputes the score per patient.

507
00:39:18.362 --> 00:39:24.591
Escalations go out when the score crosses thresholds, with suppression windows to avoid repeated pages.

508
00:39:24.871 --> 00:39:29.159
Missing vitals are handled explicitly: a stale value is not normal.

509
00:39:29.439 --> 00:39:36.970
Sepsis screens: rule-based (for example, infection suspicion plus abnormal vitals or labs) or ML-based.

510
00:39:37.250 --> 00:39:41.645
Always validated locally before activation, with alert burden measured.

511
00:39:42.995 --> 00:39:44.723
Operating theatre scheduling.

512
00:39:45.003 --> 00:39:52.299
Estimate case duration per surgeon and procedure from history (median plus spread), not from surgeon guesses alone.

513
00:39:52.579 --> 00:40:06.083
Build daily schedules as a bin-packing and sequencing problem: fit cases into theatre blocks, minimising overtime and idle time;. respect surgeon, anaesthetist, equipment and post-op bed constraints.

514
00:40:06.363 --> 00:40:11.995
Heuristics (longest-first, or first-fit decreasing) or integer programming for the weekly plan.

515
00:40:13.345 --> 00:40:19.830
Day-of-surgery re-planning when a case runs long: move later cases, and notify the ward and patients.

516
00:40:21.180 --> 00:40:24.017
Lab auto-verification and quality control.

517
00:40:24.297 --> 00:40:42.516
Rules per test: within reference and critical limits;. no instrument flags;. delta check against the patient's previous result (a large change in a short time suggests a wrong sample or a real event);. consistency across tests (for example, calculated versus measured values).

518
00:40:42.796 --> 00:40:47.148
Results passing all rules are released automatically, and the rest are held.

519
00:40:48.498 --> 00:40:53.511
Westgard QC rules on control samples detect systematic and random error.

520
00:40:53.791 --> 00:41:00.213
For example, one control beyond 3 SD, or two consecutive beyond 2 SD on the same side.

521
00:41:00.493 --> 00:41:02.989
Patient runs are blocked when QC fails.

522
00:41:04.339 --> 00:41:06.216
Claims and denial analytics.

523
00:41:06.496 --> 00:41:18.635
Pre-submission checks: a rules engine checks completeness (documents per procedure), coding consistency (diagnosis versus procedure), and policy limits (room-rent caps, sub-limits).

524
00:41:18.915 --> 00:41:20.771
This catches avoidable denials.

525
00:41:21.051 --> 00:41:28.496
Denial analytics: classify deductions by reason (text classification of payer remarks, plus codes where provided).

526
00:41:28.776 --> 00:41:31.997
Pareto analysis finds the top fixable causes.

527
00:41:32.277 --> 00:41:38.379
Prediction (optional): the likely approved amount for a pre-auth, to set patient expectations early.

528
00:41:39.729 --> 00:41:40.667
Inventory.

529
00:41:40.947 --> 00:41:51.486
Reorder point = average daily usage times lead time + safety stock, where safety stock = z times σ(usage) times √(lead time).

530
00:41:51.766 --> 00:41:52.790
Order quantity:

531
00:41:53.070 --> 00:41:58.382
EOQ as a starting point, adjusted for supplier minimums, expiry and storage.

532
00:41:58.662 --> 00:42:06.641
ABC analysis (by value) and VED analysis (vital or essential or desirable) set control intensity.

533
00:42:06.921 --> 00:42:08.905
Vital items never stock out.

534
00:42:09.185 --> 00:42:10.571
Expiry management:

535
00:42:10.851 --> 00:42:16.291
FEFO picking, and alerts N days before expiry for redistribution between hospitals.

536
00:42:17.641 --> 00:42:20.009
Access-anomaly detection (privacy).

537
00:42:20.289 --> 00:42:29.036
Rules flag risky access: access to records of patients with no care relationship (not on the user's ward, clinic list or consult);.

538
00:42:29.316 --> 00:42:35.887
VIP or employee records;. unusual volumes;. after-hours access from unusual locations.

539
00:42:36.167 --> 00:42:40.071
Scoring: a baseline per role, with z-scores on access counts.

540
00:42:40.351 --> 00:42:43.017
Flags go to a privacy officer's review queue.

541
00:42:44.367 --> 00:42:45.306
