585
00:44:04.797 --> 00:44:07.655
Tamper-evident (hash-chained), long retention.

586
00:44:09.005 --> 00:44:10.051
Event streaming.

587
00:44:10.331 --> 00:44:24.219
Kafka carries domain events: patient.registered, encounter.started, adt.admitted, order.signed, result.validated, medication.administered, bed.status.changed, charge.created, claim.status.changed.

588
00:44:24.499 --> 00:44:31.261
Outbox pattern in each service, so events are published if and only if the transaction commits (Ch 15).

589
00:44:31.541 --> 00:44:36.917
CDC (Debezium) from databases feeds the lake without touching application code.

590
00:44:38.267 --> 00:44:44.710
Consumers: read models, billing, notifications, the early-warning processor, the command centre, the lake.

591
00:44:44.990 --> 00:44:48.915
Topics with PHI are encrypted, with restricted ACLs.

592
00:44:49.195 --> 00:44:53.419
Retention is short on the bus, and long-term history lives in the lake.

593
00:44:54.769 --> 00:44:56.241
Data lake and warehouse.

594
00:44:56.521 --> 00:44:57.439
Raw zone:

595
00:44:57.719 --> 00:45:05.143
CDC and event data land in Apache Iceberg tables on object storage, partitioned by date and facility.

596
00:45:05.423 --> 00:45:15.108
Cleaned zone: deduplicated and conformed: codes mapped through the terminology service;. patients linked by enterprise ID;. time zones normalised.

597
00:45:15.388 --> 00:45:20.273
Warehouse and marts follow the Kimball approach (star schemas, Ch 21).

598
00:45:21.623 --> 00:45:30.071
Facts: encounters, admissions, orders, results, medication administrations, charges, claims, bed-days, OT cases, appointments.

599
00:45:30.351 --> 00:45:38.266
Dimensions: patient (with de-identified variants), doctor, department, facility, date, payer, diagnosis, procedure, drug.

600
00:45:38.546 --> 00:45:43.602
Slowly changing dimensions (type 2) for doctor department and tariff versions.

601
00:45:43.882 --> 00:45:44.991
Query engines:

602
00:45:45.271 --> 00:45:49.175
Trino (or Spark SQL) over Iceberg for heavy analysis;.

603
00:45:50.525 --> 00:45:56.947
a serving warehouse (ClickHouse, or a cloud warehouse if allowed by residency and policy) for dashboards;.

604
00:45:57.227 --> 00:46:01.536
Superset or similar BI with row-level security by facility.

605
00:46:01.816 --> 00:46:17.923
Typical outputs: operations: bed occupancy, average length of stay, discharge delay, OT utilisation, ED length of stay, lab TAT;. quality: infection rates, readmissions within 30 days, mortality indices, medication errors;.

606
00:46:19.273 --> 00:46:27.145
finance: revenue per bed-day, denial rate by payer;. statutory: notifiable diseases, government scheme reports.

607
00:46:27.425 --> 00:46:46.518
Research data: de-identified extracts in the OMOP Common Data Model, for approved studies, with ethics committee approval and data use agreements;. de-identification: remove direct identifiers, shift dates per patient, generalise rare attributes, and check k-anonymity risk before release.

608
00:46:47.868 --> 00:46:48.999
ML platform.

609
00:46:49.279 --> 00:46:57.748
Features are computed in the lake and served online for real-time uses (a feature store such as Feast, or simple precomputed tables).

610
00:46:58.028 --> 00:47:11.681
Models: no-show prediction, length-of-stay prediction, bed demand forecasting, readmission risk, sepsis screening (with clinical validation), denial prediction, and radiology triage (vendor, regulated).

611
00:47:11.961 --> 00:47:21.412
Governance: model cards, validation on local data, bias checks across groups;. silent mode (predictions logged but not shown) before go-live;.

612
00:47:22.762 --> 00:47:27.989
monitoring for drift;. clinical owner sign-off, and the ability to switch off instantly.

