RabbitMQ for simple queues.

695
00:50:48.827 --> 00:50:49.509
Why:

696
00:50:49.789 --> 00:50:52.541
Durable event log, replay (Ch 14).

697
00:50:52.821 --> 00:50:54.059
Stream processing.

698
00:50:54.339 --> 00:50:55.171
Choice:

699
00:50:55.451 --> 00:50:57.456
Kafka Streams or Apache Flink.

700
00:50:57.736 --> 00:50:58.419
Why:

701
00:50:58.699 --> 00:51:00.981
Early-warning scores, alerting windows.

702
00:51:02.331 --> 00:51:03.675
Integration engine.

703
00:51:03.955 --> 00:51:04.787
Choice:

704
00:51:05.067 --> 00:51:08.118
Mirth Connect or NextGen Connect or Apache Camel.

705
00:51:08.398 --> 00:51:09.422
Alternatives:

706
00:51:09.702 --> 00:51:10.918
Commercial engines.

707
00:51:11.198 --> 00:51:11.881
Why:

708
00:51:12.161 --> 00:51:14.955
HL7 v2 tooling, mapping UI.

709
00:51:15.235 --> 00:51:16.025
Imaging.

710
00:51:16.305 --> 00:51:17.137
Choice:

711
00:51:17.417 --> 00:51:22.921
Orthanc or dcm4chee PACS + OHIF web viewer; object storage VNA.

712
00:51:23.201 --> 00:51:24.225
Alternatives:

713
00:51:24.505 --> 00:51:25.593
Commercial PACS.

714
00:51:25.873 --> 00:51:26.555
Why:

715
00:51:26.835 --> 00:51:28.713
DICOM standards, web viewing.

716
00:51:28.993 --> 00:51:30.038
Terminology.

717
00:51:30.318 --> 00:51:31.150
Choice:

718
00:51:31.430 --> 00:51:34.971
Snowstorm (SNOMED CT) + local code services.

719
00:51:35.251 --> 00:51:36.275
Alternatives:

720
00:51:36.555 --> 00:51:37.409
Commercial.

721
00:51:37.689 --> 00:51:38.371
Why:

722
00:51:38.651 --> 00:51:40.635
Codes for CDS and analytics.

723
00:51:40.915 --> 00:51:41.662
Video.

724
00:51:41.942 --> 00:51:42.774
Choice:

725
00:51:43.054 --> 00:51:48.409
WebRTC with an SFU (LiveKit, mediasoup or Jitsi) + coturn TURN.

726
00:51:48.689 --> 00:51:49.713
Alternatives:

727
00:51:49.993 --> 00:51:51.635
Commercial video APIs.

728
00:51:51.915 --> 00:51:52.598
Why:

729
00:51:52.878 --> 00:51:55.054
Control over residency and cost.

730
00:51:55.334 --> 00:51:56.550
Object storage.

731
00:51:56.830 --> 00:51:57.662
Choice:

732
00:51:57.942 --> 00:52:01.483
MinIO on-prem or cloud object storage (in-country).

733
00:52:01.763 --> 00:52:02.446
Why:

734
00:52:02.726 --> 00:52:04.433
Documents, images, lake.

735
00:52:05.783 --> 00:52:06.743
Lakehouse.

736
00:52:07.023 --> 00:52:07.855
Choice:

737
00:52:08.135 --> 00:52:10.609
Apache Iceberg + Spark + Trino.

738
00:52:10.889 --> 00:52:11.913
Alternatives:

739
00:52:12.193 --> 00:52:13.452
Delta Lake, Hudi.

740
00:52:13.732 --> 00:52:14.415
Why:

741
00:52:14.695 --> 00:52:17.169
Open table formats, SQL at scale.

742
00:52:17.449 --> 00:52:18.601
Serving warehouse.

743
00:52:18.881 --> 00:52:19.713
Choice:

744
00:52:19.993 --> 00:52:23.044
ClickHouse (on-prem) or an in-country cloud warehouse.

745
00:52:23.324 --> 00:52:24.348
Alternatives:

746
00:52:24.628 --> 00:52:26.825
Snowflake or BigQuery if permitted.

747
00:52:27.105 --> 00:52:27.788
Why:

748
00:52:28.068 --> 00:52:29.284
Fast dashboards.

749
00:52:29.564 --> 00:52:30.631
Orchestration.

750
00:52:30.911 --> 00:52:31.743
Choice:

751
00:52:32.023 --> 00:52:34.967
Apache Airflow; dbt for transformations.

752
00:52:35.247 --> 00:52:36.271
Alternatives:

753
00:52:36.551 --> 00:52:37.425
Dagster.

754
00:52:37.705 --> 00:52:38.388
Why:

755
00:52:38.668 --> 00:52:41.527
Scheduled pipelines, tested SQL models.

756
00:52:41.807 --> 00:52:42.532
BI.

757
00:52:42.812 --> 00:52:43.644
Choice:

758
00:52:43.924 --> 00:52:45.225
Apache Superset.

759
00:52:45.505 --> 00:52:46.529
Alternatives:

760
00:52:46.809 --> 00:52:48.409
Power BI, Metabase.

761
00:52:48.689 --> 00:52:49.372
Why:

762
00:52:49.652 --> 00:52:51.892
Row-level security, open source.

763
00:52:52.172 --> 00:52:52.919
ML.

764
00:52:53.199 --> 00:52:54.031
Choice:

765
00:52:54.311 --> 00:52:58.236
MLflow, Python (scikit-learn, XGBoost), feature tables.

766
00:52:58.516 --> 00:52:59.540
Alternatives:

767
00:52:59.820 --> 00:53:02.231
SageMaker or Vertex (if permitted).

768
00:53:02.511 --> 00:53:03.193
Why:

769
00:53:03.473 --> 00:53:05.201
Tracking, reproducibility.

770
00:53:06.551 --> 00:53:07.490
Platform.

771
00:53:07.770 --> 00:53:08.602
Choice:

772
00:53:08.882 --> 00:53:14.791
Kubernetes (on-prem OpenShift or Rancher, or in-country cloud), Terraform, Argo CD.

773
00:53:15.071 --> 00:53:16.095
Alternatives:

774
00:53:16.375 --> 00:53:17.613
VMs for PACS.

775
00:53:17.893 --> 00:53:18.575
Why:

776
00:53:18.855 --> 00:53:21.266
Standard deployment (Ch 26).

777
00:53:21.546 --> 00:53:22.634
Observability.

778
00:53:22.914 --> 00:53:23.746
Choice:

779
00:53:24.026 --> 00:53:31.130
Prometheus, Grafana, Loki or OpenSearch logs, OpenTelemetry tracing; PHI scrubbing in logs.

780
00:53:31.410 --> 00:53:32.434
Alternatives:

781
00:53:32.714 --> 00:53:34.015
Commercial APM.

782
00:53:34.295 --> 00:53:34.978
Why:

783
00:53:35.258 --> 00:53:36.538
Ch 24.

784
00:53:36.818 --> 00:53:37.757
Security.

785
00:53:38.037 --> 00:53:38.869
Choice:

786
00:53:39.149 --> 00:53:43.437
Vault or HSM for keys, WAF, MDM for devices, SIEM.

787
00:53:43.717 --> 00:53:44.399
Why:

788
00:53:44.679 --> 00:53:45.981
Ch 23.

789
00:53:47.331 --> 00:53:48.184
Part 10.

790
00:53:48.464 --> 00:53:50.704
Security, Privacy and Compliance.

791
00:53:51.954 --> 00:53:53.106
Access control:

792
00:53:53.386 --> 00:54:02.730
RBAC (doctor, nurse, pharmacist, billing, admin), plus relationship-based rules: care team membership, ward, consult, or the referring doctor;.

793
00:54:03.010 --> 00:54:09.645
ABAC for sensitive categories (mental health, HIV, reproductive health), which get extra restrictions;.

794
00:54:09.925 --> 00:54:16.090
Break-glass: emergency access outside the rules, with a reason, alerts and after-the-fact review.

795
00:54:17.440 --> 00:54:25.333
Audit: every view, create, update, print and export of patient data is logged with user, patient, action, reason and time.

796
00:54:25.613 --> 00:54:32.120
The log is tamper-evident (hash-chained, WORM archive) and regularly reviewed (§6.9).

797
00:54:32.400 --> 00:54:33.296
Encryption:

798
00:54:33.576 --> 00:54:43.176
TLS everywhere;. encryption at rest with keys in an HSM or KMS;. field-level encryption for especially sensitive identifiers.

799
00:54:44.526 --> 00:54:50.478
Consent: consent records for treatment, data sharing (ABDM), research and teleconsultation.

800
00:54:50.758 --> 00:54:54.065
They are checked by the consent service on every outbound share.

801
00:54:54.345 --> 00:54:55.710
Data protection law:

802
00:54:55.990 --> 00:55:09.857
DPDP Act 2023 obligations, including notice, consent, purpose limitation, data principal rights, breach notification and security safeguards (VERIFY the rules issued under the Act).

803
00:55:10.137 --> 00:55:17.070
Also the clinical establishment rules and medical record retention requirements (VERIFY) (Ch 36).

804
00:55:18.420 --> 00:55:27.551
Devices: managed tablets and workstations, auto-logout, no local PHI storage, and badge tap for quick login on shared terminals.

805
00:55:27.831 --> 00:55:29.345
Medical device software:

806
00:55:29.625 --> 00:55:35.151
CDS and AI features may fall under medical device regulation (VERIFY).

807
00:55:35.431 --> 00:55:37.756
Validation and change control apply.

808
00:55:39.106 --> 00:55:40.109
Part 11.

809
00:55:40.389 --> 00:55:43.461
Reliability, Downtime and Disaster Recovery.

810
00:55:44.711 --> 00:56:00.327
Clinical core availability: active-standby databases with synchronous replication within the region (RPO about 0);. automated failover with fencing (Ch 27);. a DR site in another zone or region within the country.

811
00:56:00.607 --> 00:56:11.039
Hospital edge nodes: each hospital runs a local node with: a read-only replica of its active patients' records;. local printing of MARs and results;.

812
00:56:12.389 --> 00:56:17.957
a downtime order-entry mode whose orders queue locally and sync when the WAN returns.

813
00:56:18.237 --> 00:56:23.122
Paper downtime forms remain the last resort, and downtime drills are scheduled.

814
00:56:23.402 --> 00:56:24.810
Graceful degradation:

815
00:56:25.090 --> 00:56:31.938
If CDS is unavailable, orders proceed with a visible checks unavailable banner and pharmacist review.

816
00:56:32.218 --> 00:56:34.330
If billing is down, charges queue.

817
00:56:35.680 --> 00:56:39.733
If telemedicine video fails, it falls back to audio, then phone.

818
00:56:40.013 --> 00:56:44.557
Interface monitoring: a heartbeat and backlog alarm on every interface.

819
00:56:44.837 --> 00:56:50.789
A silent analyser interface means results aren't reaching charts, which is a patient safety risk.

820
00:56:51.069 --> 00:56:51.859
Imaging:

821
00:56:52.139 --> 00:56:58.027
PACS keeps recent studies on local storage at each hospital, with the central archive replicated.

822
00:56:58.307 --> 00:57:03.619
Older studies are recalled from the archive on demand, with pre-fetching for scheduled patients.

823
00:57:03.899 --> 00:57:07.952
Backups and restore drills for every store, with restore time measured.

824
00:57:09.302 --> 00:57:10.241
Part 12.

825
00:57:10.521 --> 00:57:12.675
Key Trade-offs (ADR summary).

826
00:57:13.925 --> 00:57:15.269
Here is a quick table:

827
00:57:15.549 --> 00:57:18.259
Decision, and Chosen, and Trade-off accepted.

828
00:57:18.539 --> 00:57:20.053
Canonical clinical model.

829
00:57:20.333 --> 00:57:21.165
Chosen:

830
00:57:21.445 --> 00:57:22.619
FHIR R4.

831
00:57:22.899 --> 00:57:24.157
Trade-off accepted:

832
00:57:24.437 --> 00:57:29.259
Some performance cost and modelling learning curve; big gain in exchange and tooling.

833
00:57:29.539 --> 00:57:30.605
Core structure.

834
00:57:30.885 --> 00:57:31.717
Chosen:

835
00:57:31.997 --> 00:57:35.731
Modular clinical core + separate operational services.

836
00:57:36.011 --> 00:57:37.269
Trade-off accepted:

837
00:57:37.549 --> 00:57:41.859
Core is larger, but clinical transactions stay simple and consistent.

838
00:57:42.139 --> 00:57:43.312
Patient matching.

839
00:57:43.592 --> 00:57:44.424
Chosen:

840
00:57:44.704 --> 00:57:47.584
Probabilistic with human review in the grey zone.

841
00:57:47.864 --> 00:57:49.123
Trade-off accepted:

842
00:57:49.403 --> 00:57:53.392
Staff effort on review queues, to avoid dangerous auto-merges.

843
00:57:53.672 --> 00:57:54.547
Alerts.

844
00:57:54.827 --> 00:57:55.659
Chosen:

845
00:57:55.939 --> 00:57:58.243
Tiered CDS with override tracking.

846
00:57:58.523 --> 00:57:59.781
Trade-off accepted:

847
00:58:00.061 --> 00:58:03.368
Some risk of missed warnings, to preserve clinician trust.

848
00:58:03.648 --> 00:58:04.565
Deployment.

849
00:58:04.845 --> 00:58:05.677
Chosen:

850
00:58:05.957 --> 00:58:08.005
In-country, hospital edge nodes.

851
00:58:08.285 --> 00:58:09.544
Trade-off accepted:

852
00:58:09.824 --> 00:58:14.667
Higher ops cost; resilience to WAN loss and residency compliance.

853
00:58:16.017 --> 00:58:17.190
Imaging storage.

854
00:58:17.470 --> 00:58:18.302
Chosen:

855
00:58:18.582 --> 00:58:19.627
Tiered VNA.

856
00:58:19.907 --> 00:58:21.166
Trade-off accepted:

857
00:58:21.446 --> 00:58:23.409
Retrieval delay for old studies.

858
00:58:23.689 --> 00:58:24.649
Analytics.

859
00:58:24.929 --> 00:58:25.761
Chosen:

860
00:58:26.041 --> 00:58:27.342
Lakehouse + OMOP.

861
00:58:27.622 --> 00:58:28.881
Trade-off accepted:

862
00:58:29.161 --> 00:58:33.555
Pipeline complexity; one source for operations, quality and research.

863
00:58:34.905 --> 00:58:35.951
Part 13.

864
00:58:36.231 --> 00:58:37.575
Interview Q and A.

865
00:58:38.825 --> 00:58:39.657
Beginner.

866
00:58:39.937 --> 00:58:40.961
Question 1.

867
00:58:41.241 --> 00:58:44.675
What are the main systems in a hospital, and how do they connect?

868
00:58:44.955 --> 00:58:46.321
Thirty-second answer:

869
00:58:46.601 --> 00:59:10.110
Patient administration (registration, ADT), scheduling, EHR with order entry, lab (LIS), radiology (RIS or PACS), pharmacy and eMAR, OT, billing and insurance, inventory, telemedicine and a patient app, connected by an integration engine (HL7 v2, FHIR) and an event bus, with ADT events telling every department where the patient is.

870
00:59:11.460 --> 00:59:12.463
Question 2.

871
00:59:12.743 --> 00:59:15.175
What happens when a doctor orders a blood test?

872
00:59:15.455 --> 00:59:16.820
Thirty-second answer:

873
00:59:17.100 --> 00:59:37.452
CPOE records a signed order with safety checks; the lab system plans samples and prints barcode labels; collection is scanned; analysers get tests by barcode and return results; auto-verification releases normal results; validated results post to the chart and notify the doctor, with critical values phoned.

874
00:59:38.802 --> 00:59:39.847
Question 3.

875
00:59:40.127 --> 00:59:42.581
How does a teleconsultation work technically?

876
00:59:42.861 --> 00:59:44.226
Thirty-second answer:

877
00:59:44.506 --> 01:00:00.058
Booking in the scheduling system, a virtual waiting room, WebRTC video through an SFU with TURN relays, adaptive bitrate and audio fallback; the doctor documents and e-prescribes in the same workspace; notes, not video, are the record.

878
01:00:01.408 --> 01:00:02.475
Question 4.

879
01:00:02.755 --> 01:00:06.509
Why is patient identity so important in hospital systems?

880
01:00:06.789 --> 01:00:08.155
Thirty-second answer:

881
01:00:08.435 --> 01:00:15.261
Duplicate records hide allergies and history; wrong merges mix two people's data, both can harm patients.

882
01:00:15.541 --> 01:00:23.755
A master patient index with probabilistic matching, human review and audited merge or unmerge keeps one person to one record.

883
01:00:25.105 --> 01:00:26.107
Intermediate.

884
01:00:26.387 --> 01:00:27.497
Question 5.

885
01:00:27.777 --> 01:00:30.977
Why use FHIR as the internal clinical data model?

886
01:00:31.257 --> 01:00:32.622
Thirty-second answer:

887
01:00:32.902 --> 01:00:50.054
It's an international standard with well-defined resources (Patient, Encounter, Observation, MedicationRequest...), versioning and search, used for national exchange (ABDM) and apps (SMART on FHIR), so exchange becomes mapping and the ecosystem of tools applies.

888
01:00:51.404 --> 01:00:52.513
Question 6.

889
01:00:52.793 --> 01:00:56.313
How does bedside medication administration prevent errors?

890
01:00:56.593 --> 01:00:57.959
Thirty-second answer:

891
01:00:58.239 --> 01:01:12.660
Pharmacist-verified orders generate an eMAR schedule; the nurse scans the wristband and the drug barcode; the system checks right patient, drug, dose, route and time before recording; exceptions need reasons.

892
01:01:14.010 --> 01:01:15.098
Question 7.

893
01:01:15.378 --> 01:01:17.981
How do you prevent double-booking of doctor slots?

894
01:01:18.261 --> 01:01:19.626
Thirty-second answer:

895
01:01:19.906 --> 01:01:34.754
Slots generated from roster templates; a short hold with TTL during checkout; a unique constraint on doctor-slot-sequence with a conditional insert; waitlist fill on cancellations; overbooking only by explicit policy.

896
01:01:36.104 --> 01:01:37.128
Question 8.

897
01:01:37.408 --> 01:01:41.013
What is an integration engine and why does a hospital need one?

898
01:01:41.293 --> 01:01:42.659
Thirty-second answer:

899
01:01:42.939 --> 01:02:02.651
It translates and routes messages (HL7 v2, ASTM, FHIR, DICOM metadata) between analysers, monitors, departmental systems, insurers and national platforms, with mapping, retries, replay and backlog monitoring, because hospitals run many systems that were never designed together.

900
01:02:04.001 --> 01:02:05.067
Question 9.

901
01:02:05.347 --> 01:02:07.779
How does cashless insurance work in the system?

902
01:02:08.059 --> 01:02:09.425
Thirty-second answer:

903
01:02:09.705 --> 01:02:27.774
Eligibility check at admission, a pre-authorisation request with diagnosis and estimate via payer API or claims exchange, queries turned into tasks, enhancements for longer stays, final claim with documents, remittance reconciliation and denial analysis.

904
01:02:29.124 --> 01:02:29.892
Senior.

905
01:02:30.172 --> 01:02:31.217
Question 10.

906
01:02:31.497 --> 01:02:33.396
Design the master patient index.

907
01:02:33.676 --> 01:02:35.041
Thirty-second answer:

908
01:02:35.321 --> 01:02:56.655
Blocking keys to limit comparisons; field comparators (Jaro, Winkler and phonetic codes for names, DOB variants, mobile, ABHA); Fellegi, Sunter weights from reviewed pairs; auto-link above an upper threshold, human review in the grey zone, new record below; audited merge or unmerge events that downstream systems follow.

909
01:02:58.005 --> 01:02:59.135
Question 11.

910
01:02:59.415 --> 01:03:03.042
How would you implement early-warning alerts from bedside monitors?

911
01:03:03.322 --> 01:03:04.687
Thirty-second answer:

912
01:03:04.967 --> 01:03:24.807
Device gateway converts monitor data to Observations; a stream processor keeps per-patient state and computes the approved score (for example, NEWS2) on each vital set; threshold crossings trigger escalation with suppression windows; stale data is surfaced, not treated as normal; alert burden is measured.

913
01:03:26.157 --> 01:03:27.245
Question 12.

914
01:03:27.525 --> 01:03:31.024
How do you store and serve medical images at petabyte scale?

915
01:03:31.304 --> 01:03:32.669
Thirty-second answer:

916
01:03:32.949 --> 01:03:51.253
PACS for DICOM ingest and worklists, a vendor-neutral archive on tiered object storage with lossless compression and lifecycle rules, pre-fetching priors for scheduled patients, DICOMweb for zero-footprint viewing that streams only needed frames, and local caches per hospital.

917
01:03:52.603 --> 01:03:53.883
Question 13.

918
01:03:54.163 --> 01:03:58.089
How do you keep clinicians working when the network or data centre fails?

919
01:03:58.369 --> 01:03:59.734
Thirty-second answer:

920
01:04:00.014 --> 01:04:15.267
Hospital edge nodes with read-only replicas of active patients, local printing of MARs and results, a downtime order-entry mode that queues and syncs later, paper procedures as last resort, and scheduled downtime drills.

921
01:04:16.617 --> 01:04:17.812
Question 14.

922
01:04:18.092 --> 01:04:20.929
How do you design access control for patient records?

923
01:04:21.209 --> 01:04:22.575
Thirty-second answer:

924
01:04:22.855 --> 01:04:40.647
Roles plus care relationships (team, ward, consult) and sensitivity labels for special categories; break-glass with reason and review; every access audited in a tamper-evident log; anomaly detection on access patterns; minimum necessary in reports and exports.

925
01:04:41.997 --> 01:04:42.893
Architect.

926
01:04:43.173 --> 01:04:44.367
Question 15.

927
01:04:44.647 --> 01:04:47.293
Design the data platform for a hospital chain.

928
01:04:47.573 --> 01:04:48.938
Thirty-second answer:

929
01:04:49.218 --> 01:05:11.021
Outbox events and CDC into Kafka; an Iceberg lakehouse with raw and conformed zones (codes mapped, patients linked); Kimball star-schema marts for operations, quality and finance; OMOP de-identified store for research; feature tables and an ML platform with governance; BI with facility-level row security; all in-country.

930
01:05:12.371 --> 01:05:13.565
Question 16.

931
01:05:13.845 --> 01:05:16.917
How would you introduce a sepsis prediction model safely?

932
01:05:17.197 --> 01:05:18.563
Thirty-second answer:

933
01:05:18.843 --> 01:05:36.613
Local validation on retrospective data, bias checks, silent mode with outcome comparison, a clinical owner and response protocol, measured alert burden and outcomes after go-live, drift monitoring and an off switch, treated as regulated clinical software where applicable.

934
01:05:37.963 --> 01:05:39.307
Question 17.

935
01:05:39.587 --> 01:05:41.785
How do you integrate a hospital with ABDM?

936
01:05:42.065 --> 01:05:43.430
Thirty-second answer:

937
01:05:43.710 --> 01:06:04.147
Register facility and professionals, link ABHA to MRNs with patient consent, act as HIP or HIU through an ABDM gateway, serve FHIR bundles only against valid consent artefacts (encrypted to the requester), honour revocations, and log every exchange, mapping from the internal FHIR model.

938
01:06:05.497 --> 01:06:06.671
Question 18.

939
01:06:06.951 --> 01:06:10.001
How would you reduce discharge delays across the chain?

940
01:06:10.281 --> 01:06:11.647
Thirty-second answer:

941
01:06:11.927 --> 01:06:30.636
Measure the time from medically-fit to bed-free by step, start discharge planning at admission (predicted LOS), run insurance and billing tasks in parallel with clinical steps, automate discharge summaries from structured data, trigger housekeeping on discharge events, and show a live discharge board.

942
01:06:31.986 --> 01:06:33.223
Question 19.

943
01:06:33.503 --> 01:06:35.189
Buy a commercial HIS or build?

944
01:06:35.469 --> 01:06:36.706
How would you decide?

945
01:06:36.986 --> 01:06:38.351
Thirty-second answer:

946
01:06:38.631 --> 01:06:56.871
Compare total cost, clinical workflow fit, standards support (FHIR, HL7, DICOM, ABDM), vendor lock-in and data access, regulatory burden, and team capacity; many chains buy the clinical core and build patient apps, integration, data platform and analytics around it.

947
01:06:58.221 --> 01:06:59.331
Question 20.

948
01:06:59.611 --> 01:07:04.240
What are the top risks in hospital software, and how does the architecture address them?

949
01:07:04.520 --> 01:07:05.885
Thirty-second answer:

950
01:07:06.165 --> 01:07:27.435
Wrong-patient data (MPI, barcodes, delta checks), missed critical information (CDS, critical value workflows, early warnings), downtime (edge nodes, drills), privacy breaches (access rules, audit, anomaly detection), silent interface failures (heartbeats, backlogs) and unsafe AI (validation, silent mode, governance).

951
01:07:28.785 --> 01:07:29.830
Part 14.

952
01:07:30.110 --> 01:07:30.878
Summary.

953
01:07:32.128 --> 01:07:43.115
A hospital runs on identity, events and records: one patient, one record (MPI); ADT events move every department; immutable, signed clinical data.

954
01:07:43.395 --> 01:07:49.709
FHIR as the canonical model makes the EHR, apps and national exchange (ABDM) one language.

955
01:07:49.989 --> 01:07:52.101
Safety is designed in at every step:

956
01:07:52.381 --> 01:08:00.872
CDS at order time, barcode administration, delta checks and Westgard QC, early-warning streams, critical-value workflows.

957
01:08:02.222 --> 01:08:12.590
Operations run on algorithms: probabilistic matching, bed assignment and forecasting, overbooking from no-show prediction, OT bin-packing, reorder points.

958
01:08:12.870 --> 01:08:23.195
The data platform (events, then lakehouse, then marts, OMOP, ML) serves operations, quality, finance and research, with de-identification and governance.

959
01:08:23.475 --> 01:08:30.622
Resilience is local: hospital edge nodes, downtime procedures and drills, because clinical care cannot pause.

